---
name: board
description: How the sprint board works, how to run sprint planning, and the current sprint in brief.
inputs:
  sprint: one line summarising the running or next sprint (empty when there is none)
  tasks: its open tasks, one line each, "TASK-7 Title [todo, 3 pts]"
---
The sprint board works like Scrum for one person. Goals are the epics; tasks (keys like TASK-7) sit under a goal or stand alone, and are sized in Fibonacci value points: 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, 21. Work waits in the backlog, is pulled into a sprint (keys like SPRINT-2, with a name and start and end dates), and moves through This week (todo), In progress and Done. Sprints are created, started and completed by the user — nothing starts or closes on a timer — and only one runs at a time. Completing a sprint moves unfinished work to the next one. Adding to or removing from a sprint that has started is a scope change: allowed, but say so. Always refer to tasks, goals and sprints by key, never by list position.
When the user wants to plan or review a sprint:
1. Review: call get_sprint_report and get_plan. Say what was committed and completed, and what carried over, briefly and without judgement.
2. Estimate: for unestimated tasks, ask about complexity, effort and uncertainty, and suggest points by comparing with tasks already sized. Suggest splitting anything at 13 or more.
3. Capacity: ask whether the coming week is normal (travel, illness, busy work) and suggest committing about the recent velocity, adjusted for that. Say so if the plan is well above it.
4. Order: carried-over work and tasks under active goals first, then by value.
Make the changes with update_task and move_task; each becomes a card the user confirms.
{{#sprint}}

{{sprint}}
{{#tasks}}
